# Chapter 1 – Riches to Rags

*Banaras, before dawn.*

Banaras breathed like a sleeping giant as the first gold ribbons of sunlight unfurled across the Ganga. Before the streets awoke, the holy river shimmered gently, reflecting fishermen leaning into their nets and boatmen rowing pilgrims across the foggy current. Priests, wrapped in saffron shawls, recited verses that melted into the cool morning air, their chants drifting over the ghats like sacred smoke.

Soon the city stirred  vendors calling prices of guavas and marigolds, women bargaining loudly, the metallic clang of copper pots, children running barefoot through narrow lanes. The market of Banaras hummed with life, a mosaic of color and noise.

Then came the royal horns.

At once, shopkeepers paused mid sale, priests paused mid mantra, and the crowd parted instinctively. A procession of guards emerged from the dust clouded street, their polished spears catching the sun. Behind them, elevated atop a magnificent black stallion, rode **Raja Chait Singh of Banaras**, his posture regal yet burdened.

Four soldiers followed, carrying the spoils of the Raja’s early hunt  an enormous stag, its antlers spreading like a crown of the wild. Children gasped, running alongside the entourage. Elders bowed. The Raja acknowledged them with a quiet nod, his expression gentle, betraying none of the storms brewing within the palace walls.

As he crossed through the bustling market and into the palace gates, the cheers faded. The façade of a peaceful kingdom ended at the courtyard.

---

## **The Courtyard and the Prince**

The palace courtyard glistened with dew as Naik Singh , thirteen, lean, curious, and unbearably proud  hovered over the marble fountain. He studied his reflection intently. The water shimmered back the face he loved to imagine as royal, destined, unconquerable.

He drew himself up straighter.

*“Again?”* his friend and playmate, a servant boy only a year older, asked.

Naik flicked his chin upward like a seasoned king.

*“I am the prince. So again. You bow.”*

The servant boy obeyed, kneeling with exaggerated reluctance. Naik grinned, satisfied at the perfection of the moment. He loved these games where he was ruler, powerful, unquestioned. Games where the world bent at his command.

Beyond the courtyard, he heard the distant hum of the Raja’s Sabha beginning its morning deliberations, the faint arguments of ministers, and the heavy footsteps of British officers who barged in without respect or invitation.

Naik clenched his jaw.

He hated them , the smug intruders, the disrespect, the way his uncle bowed to them despite being king. His childish heart felt the sting of humiliation deeper than he understood.

---

## **Sabha of Burdens**

In the great hall, Raja Chait Singh sat surrounded by ministers, generals, and advisors. Incense curled around sandstone pillars. Mahid Singh stood at his right  a towering figure at almost thirty, battle scarred and fiercely respected.

British officers stepped arrogantly forward, parchment in hand.

*“An order,”* one announced, *“from Governor Warren Hastings. Taxes will increase again. Provisions will be supplied for Company troops immediately.”*

Murmurs rippled through the hall.

A minister protested, *“My lord, our people are starving  ”*

The officer cut him off.

*“It is not a suggestion.”*

The very stones of the Sabha felt insulted.

But this time, Raja Chait Singh did not argue. He bowed his head, the gesture small but humiliating.

Mahid’s jaw hardened.

Naik, observing from a far corner as a page, felt his hands curl into fists.

When the British left, silence lingered like a stain. Ministers whispered anxiously. The Raja dismissed them for the day.

But Mahid did not leave.

---

## **Mahid Singh’s Secret Plan**

Late that afternoon, Mahid gathered a select few  three generals, two senior advisors, and three ministers he believed were loyal to his father’s line.

He shut the doors.

*“We cannot live like animals under foreign boots,”* he began quietly. His voice carried the weight of a soldier who had buried too many men. *“Our king bends to survive. I cannot fault him, but I refuse to watch Banaras decay into a colony of cowards.”*

One advisor spoke hesitantly.

*“What are you proposing, Mahid Babu?”*

He took a breath.

*“We kill Warren Hastings.”*

The room stiffened.

Mahid laid out a plan with strategic precision:

A diplomatic feast the following week. Poisoned wine. Swift execution. British chaos. Sealing the city gates. Rallying neighboring kingdoms to join the uprising.

A desperate plan. A brave plan. A doomed plan.

But Mahid believed in it. He believed the ministers would stand with him. He believed Banaras was ready to reclaim dignity.

Blind trust would cost him everything.

For that night, under the silence of the palace lanterns, two ministers slipped out of a side gate and disappeared into the British camp. Their pockets jingled with the first payment of their betrayal.

By dawn, Hastings knew every detail  Mahid’s plan, the palace layout, the weak points of the guards.

This was the excuse the Company needed to wipe Banaras off the map.

---

## **The Day Before the Fall**

The next day unfolded strangely peacefully. The Raja held Sabha as usual, unaware of the storm forming at his doorstep. Mahid spent the afternoon finalizing details with his generals, feeling a surge of hope.

Naik, restless, slipped away to the river.

He crouched at the Ganga’s edge and stared into the gentle ripples.

*“I will be king one day,”* he whispered to himself.

*“And I will never bow to anyone.”*

His reflection wavered.

Even at thirteen, pride burned dangerously bright in him.

But as he watched the water, he felt a strange heaviness in the air  a quiet omen he could not name.

---

## **The Gates Open at Midnight**

Midnight cloaked Banaras in a fragile calm. Clouds swallowed the moon. Even the dogs slept uneasily.

At the eastern gate, two familiar ministers approached, their lanterns swaying.

*“Open it,”* one whispered to the guards, handing them forged orders bearing the Raja’s seal.

The guards obeyed.

The gate groaned open.

And hell entered Banaras.

Rows of British soldiers marched silently into the city. Bayonets gleamed. Boots crushed the dirt paths. Muskets were loaded but silent.

Their mission clear:

**Eliminate Raja Chait Singh and Mahid Singh.**

The genocide had begun.

---

## **Fire and Screams**

The attack was swift and merciless.

Gunshots shattered the night.

Houses ignited.

Families fled into alleys only to be cornered by soldiers.

Temples  places that had heard centuries of prayers  echoed instead with screams.

Inside the palace, chaos erupted. Guards attempted to defend the gates, but the traitors had already sabotaged the defenses. Muskets fired relentlessly. British troops advanced like a dark tide.

Naik, awakened by explosions, stumbled out onto the veranda just in time to see the palace gates burst open with a thunderous blast.

He froze  terror flooding every vein.

A guard seized him. *“Prince! Come quickly!”*

Naik and the other boys were rushed through corridors heavy with smoke and blood. They were pushed into a small stone chamber behind the kitchen.

*“Stay silent,”* the guard said.

*“I will protect you.”*

But outside, nothing was left to protect.

---

## **The Throne Room of Death**

The hall of Banaras, once a place of counsel and justice, had become a butcher’s chamber.

Bodies of ministers and loyal guards lay scattered across the blood slick floor, their lifeless faces twisted in terror. Smoke drifted through shattered windows. Flames crawled up embroidered banners bearing the royal emblem. The throne  once a symbol of dignity  stood crooked and cracked at its base.

British soldiers lined the room in a merciless ring, rifles ready, bayonets red. They were not here for negotiations. They were here for extermination.

Mahid Singh and Raja Chait Singh were dragged inside, bound and beaten, thrown onto the cold stone like cattle brought for slaughter.

The Governor himself stepped forward, boots polished, expression bored  almost amused.

Mahid lifted his bruised face and stared at him with burning defiance. Even kneeling, he radiated a warrior’s pride.

The Governor leaned in.

*“So this is the savage who planned to kill me?”*

Mahid spat directly into his face.

The troops erupted in fury. The Governor flinched back, wiping his cheek with disgust.

*“You filthy shits.”*

He struck Mahid across the jaw with his musket, knocking him sideways. Blood pooled at Mahid’s lips, but he still laughed  a broken, fearless laugh.

The Governor signaled sharply.

A soldier seized Raja Chait Singh by the hair and yanked his head upward. The Raja was trembling violently, not from pain  but from fear so deep it hollowed him out.

The Governor smirked.

*“Kneel properly, Chait Singh.”*

The Raja hesitated.

Another blow came  this time to his ribs.

He collapsed fully, forehead slamming against the stone.

*“Now,”* the Governor taunted, *“show your loyalty to the Empire.”*

A British soldier pressed his boot against the Raja’s cheek.

*“Lick it.”*

The hall went dead silent.

Naik watched from the corner where he had been shoved, shaking uncontrollably. His lungs felt crushed. This was his uncle  the man the city revered  reduced to something beneath human.

The Raja’s voice cracked.

*“Please… no… please…”*

The soldier shoved harder.

*“Lick.”*

Chait Singh’s dignity shattered.

With shaking lips, tears streaming down his face, he bent forward and licked the mud caked leather of the British soldier’s shoe.

Naik let out a strangled gasp.

Mahid closed his eyes in agony.

The Governor turned to his officers.

*“Kill them both.”*

A rifle cocked.

*“No!”* the Raja cried, crawling forward desperately.

The Governor paused.

His eyes gleamed with cruelty.

*“Oh, but you will do something for us.”*

He gestured to Mahid.

*“You will kill him. With your own hands.”*

Chait Singh froze.

Mahid’s eyes widened  not in fear, but in heartbreak.

The Raja shook violently, collapsing into sobs.

*“I cannot… he is my blood… he is my son… Please  I beg you  anything else!”*

A musket aimed directly at Naik’s head.

Chait Singh broke.

They placed a sword in his trembling hands. His fingers slipped on the hilt, slick with sweat and despair.

Mahid lifted his chin.

*“Uncle….”*

Tears streamed down both their faces.

*“I am proud of you, Mahid,”* the Raja whispered, choking on grief.

*“Forgive me… forgive me…”*

With a final shuddering breath, Raja Chait Singh drove the sword into Mahid’s chest.

The blade slid through flesh and bone with a sickening sound.

Mahid gasped  once  and fell forward into his uncle’s arms.

Naik screamed his name until his voice broke.

The Raja held Mahid’s collapsing body, rocking it, sobbing uncontrollably  his hands stained with the blood of the boy he had raised as a son.

The Governor clapped mockingly.

*“How touching.”*

The soldiers raised their rifles.

*“No  no  NO  ”* the Raja cried, reaching upward  

A volley of gunfire thundered through the hall.

Raja Chait Singh slumped backward, lifeless, falling across Mahid’s body.

The last hope of Banaras died beside the last warrior who defended it.

Naik’s world ended in a heartbeat.

---

## **The Black Waters**

By the time the sun rose, Banaras was ash. Smoke curled upward like mourning banners.

Naik, bound and trembling, was dragged onto a ship waiting at the river port. British officers watched him with cold satisfaction  as if he were nothing more than spoils of war.

He stared back at the city one last time  the temples, the ghats, the palace towers disappearing behind a veil of smoke.

He tried to stand tall, but grief crushed his spine.

He tried to breathe, but betrayal choked him.

He tried to be a prince, but he was already being erased.

A sailor shoved him toward the lower deck.

Naik stumbled into darkness.

His tears mingled with the salty sting of the ocean wind.

He felt his pride die, piece by piece.

By the time the ship pushed into the deep waters, Naik Singh  heir of Banaras was no longer a prince.

He was a prisoner.

A hostage.

And somewhere deep within him, a seed of rage took root, quiet, potent, patient.

The world had taken everything from him.

One day, he would take something back.
